--TEST--
Core\Jwt::verifyIssued refuses every header parameter that would make a verifier fetch, decompress or trust something the token brought, and ignores every hint it does not read — `rule:security/protocol-roster`, `rule:security/algorithm-comes-from-the-key`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Invariance over a sweep, asserted by counting. Every parameter below is one
// a real issuer's library can write and a verifier must not honour: a key or a
// fetch the token carries with it, an extension a verifier would have to
// understand to be safe, and a payload that is not the one segment it looks
// like. Each token here is *correctly signed by the right key*, so nothing but
// the header policy can refuse it — a member that read the list and forgot one
// entry still prints a plausible refusal for the other seven.
Core\Crypto\KeyPair $issuer = Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair(Core\Crypto\KeyKind::Ed25519);

var $segment = fn (string $text): string => Core\Encoding::toBase64Url(
    Core\Encoding::encodeText($text, Core\Charset::Utf8)
);
var $signed = fn (string $input, Core\Crypto\KeyPair $key): string => $input . "."
    . Core\Encoding::toBase64Url(
        Core\Crypto::sign(Core\Encoding::encodeText($input, Core\Charset::Utf8), $key)
    );

int $now = Core\Time::now()->toEpochSeconds();
string $payload = "{\"iss\":\"https://issuer.example\",\"aud\":\"novis-app\",\"exp\":"
    . (($now + 900) as string) . "}";

array<string> $carried = [
    "\"jku\":\"https://issuer.example/jwks.json\"",
    "\"x5u\":\"https://issuer.example/cert.pem\"",
    "\"x5c\":[\"MIIBkTCB+w==\"]",
    "\"jwk\":{\"kty\":\"OKP\",\"crv\":\"Ed25519\",\"x\":\"AAAA\"}",
    "\"crit\":[\"exp\"]",
    "\"b64\":false",
    "\"zip\":\"DEF\"",
    "\"cty\":\"JWT\"",
];

int $honoured = 0;
int $refused = 0;
string $says = "";
foreach ($carried as string $parameter) {
    string $header = "{\"alg\":\"EdDSA\"," . $parameter . ",\"typ\":\"JWT\"}";
    string $token = $signed($segment($header) . "." . $segment($payload), $issuer);
    try {
        var $claims = Core\Jwt::verifyIssued<tainted {iss: string, aud: string, exp: int}>(
            $token,
            $issuer->publicKey(),
            "https://issuer.example",
            "novis-app"
        );
        $honoured = $honoured + 1;
    } catch (RuntimeError $no) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
        $says = $no->message;
    }
}
echo $honoured, " honoured and ", $refused, " refused", "\n";

// The other side of the same bound, and the reason the list is a deny-list
// rather than an allow-list: an issuer sends hints a verifier does not read —
// `x5t` is a thumbprint of a certificate nobody here fetches — and refusing
// what is merely unknown refuses real ID tokens.
string $hinted = "{\"alg\":\"EdDSA\",\"x5t\":\"dGh1bWI\",\"typ\":\"JWT\"}";
var $ignored = Core\Jwt::verifyIssued<tainted {iss: string, aud: string, exp: int}>(
    $signed($segment($hinted) . "." . $segment($payload), $issuer),
    $issuer->publicKey(),
    "https://issuer.example",
    "novis-app"
);
echo "a hint it does not read is ", $ignored->iss, "'s to send", "\n";
echo $says, "\n";
--EXPECT--
0 honoured and 8 refused
a hint it does not read is https://issuer.example's to send
Core\Jwt::verifyIssued(): $token is not a token this issuer's key signed. Every way of not being one — a shape that is not three base64url parts, a header carrying a parameter a verifier may not honour, a `kid` that names no key in the set, an `alg` that is not the one that key carries, a signature under another key, and an altered payload — is this one sentence, so a forgery says nothing about which half of it failed.
